"Look around you. The lights on the ceiling, the traffic light at the corner, the big screen at the station, and the tiny light that tells you a charger is working are probably all LEDs. LED stands for light-emitting diode. And here is the surprise: an LED is a semiconductor, a close cousin of the chips in this series.": "7e642566eb52",
"Look around you. The lights on the ceiling, the traffic light at the corner, the big screen at the station, and the tiny light that tells you a charger is working are probably all LEDs. LED stands for light-emitting diode. And here is the surprise: an LED is a semiconductor, a close cousin of the chips in this series. In Lesson 1 you saw that adding a few other atoms to a semiconductor changes it. One kind of atom gives spare electrons and makes n-type material. Another kind leaves empty places called holes and makes p-type material. An LED is simply a layer of n-type and a layer of p-type joined together. The place where they touch is called the junction. Connect a battery the right way, and it pushes the electrons and the holes toward the junction. When an electron meets a hole, it drops in. As it drops, it gives up some energy, and that energy leaves as a tiny packet of light called a photon. A huge number of these, one after another, make the glow you see. What about the color? It depends on how big the energy step is. A bigger step makes bluer light, and a smaller step makes redder light. The size of the step is fixed by the material, so engineers choose different semiconductors for different colors. The plastic cover does not decide the color. The crystal inside does. Red LEDs came first, in the 1960s. Blue was much harder, and it took until the early 1990s. That mattered a great deal. With red, green, and blue together, a screen can show every color. And a blue LED covered with a yellow material called phosphor gives white light, the kind that now lights our homes. An old light bulb works by making a thin wire so hot that it glows, and most of its energy is lost as heat. An LED turns electricity into light directly, so it uses less power, makes less waste heat, and lasts longer. The next time you switch on a lamp, remember: you are using a semiconductor.": "706a0c08aad5",
